00:09:35.723 --> 00:09:36.556
your maiden name.

00:09:36.556 --> 00:09:37.389
What's your maiden name?

00:09:37.389 --> 00:09:38.222
- No, my maiden name Brown

00:09:38.222 --> 00:09:39.240
but my grandfather changed his name

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.113
when he came to this country.
- Of course

00:09:40.113 --> 00:09:42.220
'cause he was like Dimitry Buchabof

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:43.923
and he's like Brown.

00:09:43.923 --> 00:09:47.510
By the way, really funny thing
about having VaynerMedia,

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:48.840
this whole Vayner thing that I'm building

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:50.120
every company with it,

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:52.140
my dad when he came to America,

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:55.101
immigration tried to get
him to drop the Chuk.

00:09:55.101 --> 00:09:56.962
They're like, "You
should be Sasha Vayner."

00:09:56.962 --> 00:09:59.622
And he was like, "No, I'm Vaynerchuk."

00:09:59.622 --> 00:10:01.825
- Oh, you should name your next kid Chuck.

00:10:01.825 --> 00:10:03.900
- I wish I could have another kid.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:05.440
My wife is so against it.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.520
- Oh, get me to your wife one more time.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:08.353
- We will.

00:10:08.353 --> 00:10:10.910
- I was 41 when I had
the baby who's now 20.

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:12.240
- That's fucking ama--

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:14.460
- It was amazing, it was.
- That's exactly right.

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:17.380
- And I wish I would've done a fourth.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:18.213
No one ever says,

00:10:18.213 --> 00:10:19.750
"Oh, I wish I didn't have that kid."

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:22.750
- My dad is the most
interesting guy on this issue.

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:23.700
He's really kind of like,

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:24.660
I call him a cactus

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:25.600
'cause he's tough on the outside

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:26.650
but he's a real kind of mush

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:27.920
if you really get to know him.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:29.630
His great regret in life

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:32.221
is he promised my mom
we would have five kids,

00:10:32.221 --> 00:10:33.054
that was the only thing my mom said,

00:10:33.054 --> 00:10:34.580
they were like 19 in Russia.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:35.470
She's like, "I want five kids."

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:36.340
He's like, "Cool."

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:37.540
We had three.

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:40.140
My mom was 31 when she had AJ.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:41.483
She was a kid still.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:46.059
And my dad was so scared
about paying for college

00:10:46.059 --> 00:10:48.510
that he didn't want to have more kids

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.930
and he sits and talks
about it all the time,

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:51.950
the regret of like--

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:52.890
- But three is amazing.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:54.374
I have three.

00:10:54.374 --> 00:10:55.960
- Three's the best.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:56.793
- And you have two

00:10:56.793 --> 00:10:58.830
and your wife is so awesome, come on.

00:10:58.830 --> 00:10:59.760
- You're preaching.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:01.485
My parents are like, you
make such great kids,

00:11:01.485 --> 00:11:02.318
you need to have more.

00:11:02.318 --> 00:11:03.500
I'm like, I'm on board.

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:05.340
- Mostly I want to interview your wife

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:08.620
'cause what is it like
being married to Gary

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:11.210
that is always on, can't
go anywhere in public

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:14.524
without you being completely fanned over.

00:11:14.524 --> 00:11:15.965
How does she deal with that?

00:11:15.965 --> 00:11:18.440
- Easily, she's incredible.

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:19.670
She lives in her own cocoon.

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:20.640
I think one thing you may know

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:23.100
is we don't put out
content of her or our kids.

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:25.590
And so we play that pretty insular.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.800
She doesn't give a shit
about any of this attention.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:30.274
She's proud of me,

00:11:30.274 --> 00:11:32.100
she knows me so well

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:35.700
and one of the people that knows
me the best if not the best

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:38.230
so she knows it's coming
from a good place,

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:39.690
so she likes that.

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:41.550
But she gets scared, she gets scared,

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:42.750
she definitely gets scared.

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:44.527
And so that's something
I think about as well.

00:11:44.527 --> 00:11:46.380
- But when do you give her her time?

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:47.780
When is it just you and her?

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:51.223
- Weekends, seven weeks vacation

00:11:51.223 --> 00:11:54.930
but the truth is we're
very much a 1957 couple.

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:57.500
It is absolutely a little
bit of a divide and conquer.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:11:58.520
And we talk about that.

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.631
We know that that's something
that may have to evolve.

00:12:01.631 --> 00:12:04.120
But it is definitely 1957.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:05.700
- Meaning she's a stay-at-home mom,

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:08.190
you work and she makes sure
there's food on the table?

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:10.900
- Well no because I get home at 11:30 PM.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:12.670
- You get home at 11:30 every night?

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:14.600
- I got home at 12:37 last night.

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:15.934
- When do you see your children?

00:12:15.934 --> 00:12:16.810
I know you--

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:18.891
- Weekends, seven weeks vacation.

00:12:18.891 --> 00:12:23.890
I'm absolutely not living
a politically correct

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:26.800
to everybody's work/life
balance standpoint

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.460
but I'm also unbelievably unphased by it

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:33.060
because I'm in a depth versus
width conversation in my life

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:35.746
and I'm also in, this
is this moment right now

00:12:35.746 --> 00:12:39.170
and I'm always in a place of understanding

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:41.020
what will I regret,

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:42.080
how will I roll

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:43.020
and most importantly,

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.697
it's interesting, Lizzy
got me a plaque that said,

00:12:46.697 --> 00:12:49.275
"My father taught me
how to live by living."

00:12:49.275 --> 00:12:51.260
And I think a lot about that.

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:56.260
I think a lot about what
check boxes do I want to check

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:57.588
and for whom?

00:12:57.588 --> 00:12:59.770
The most fascinating thing to go,

00:12:59.770 --> 00:13:00.603
one of the reasons I love

00:13:00.603 --> 00:13:02.170
when the four of us went out to dinner,

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:03.320
I remember when we left,

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:05.540
I was like, see, that's
the people we kind of need

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:06.430
to hang out with

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:08.130
because what's so
interesting to have dinner

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:09.505
with couples and things of--

00:13:09.505 --> 00:13:11.030
- That was my husband and with Gary--

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:11.863
- Lizzy and all that.

00:13:11.863 --> 00:13:12.696
- Yep.

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:17.030
- Everybody's just judging everybody.

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:19.760
The amount of six person dinners I go to

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:21.677
and people are like, "But
you're supposed to do this

00:13:21.677 --> 00:13:22.730
"and you're supposed to do,"

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:25.447
I'm like, you don't know
anything about anything.

00:13:25.447 --> 00:13:27.240
I don't want to judge,

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:29.120
I put out a lot of opinions

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:30.230
but they're macro.

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:31.710
They're not to DRock.

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:34.350
They're to the general public.

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:37.390
But I have nothing for
anybody on an individual basis

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:38.560
unless they ask.

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:42.270
I have no interest in judging
people's relationships

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:44.260
or how they raise their kids

00:13:44.260 --> 00:13:45.780
or things of that nature

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:48.140
and I'm fascinated by how
many people like doing that.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:49.426
- Then what are you curious about?

00:13:49.426 --> 00:13:51.100
- Everything.

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:53.180
I'm unbelievably curious

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:55.432
about how people make decisions.

00:13:55.432 --> 00:13:56.410
No question

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:59.160
which makes me then curious
about almost everything.

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:01.760
Communication, hence Vayner.

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:05.280
I'm very fascinated by what
gets people to do things,

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:06.740
inspires them to do things,

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:09.340
makes them not do things, comms.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:12.500
I'm a boy that was born
in the Soviet Union

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:15.080
that came to America in the '80s.

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:17.790
I'm unbelievably, I think affected by that

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.650
when it comes to communication.

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:22.330
The fact that my mom wrote a book report

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:23.590
that she thought Fidel Castro

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:26.750
was the bravest man in
the world, blows my mind.

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:27.583
I'm like, wow.

00:14:27.583 --> 00:14:28.970
And then so much of what's happening

00:14:28.970 --> 00:14:30.390
in the political climate now

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.400
and with the internet and
things of that nature,

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:36.600
my life has always circled
around communication.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.850
- Do you ever think of yourself at 70?

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:39.740
- A lot.

00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:41.870
- And what do you think
you'll be doing at 70?

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:43.940
- Working a lot,

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:45.372
enjoying,

00:14:45.372 --> 00:14:47.450
very similar version of what I am now,

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:49.570
enjoying whatever that may be.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:51.540
My intuition is that I will evolve

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:54.040
into other things that
excite me and interest me

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:55.623
around communication.

00:14:56.690 --> 00:14:59.440
I'm very quiet about my non-profit work

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:00.680
or some of the other stuff I do.

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:01.930
- Oh, could you tell me about it then?

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:03.170
What's your non-profit work?

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:06.280
- So I'm on the board
of Pencils of Promise.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:08.240
I'm a well member at Charity Water

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:11.150
and I'm, laughing 'cause
I see Tyler over me,

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:13.743
I'm unbelievably fascinated by this,

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:19.580
people that donate to
causes or sit on boards

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:24.320
but then don't help acquaintances
or slight family members

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:26.580
or friends in times of need.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:28.890
So my buckets break
into I'm very passionate

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:30.710
about Charity Water and Pencils of Promise

00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:32.600
because they were founded and run

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:35.130
and not run anymore on Pencils of Promise

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:37.140
but at the time when I joined them,

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.630
they were founded and
run by young individuals

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:42.670
that were treating them more
like start-ups than NGOs

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:45.080
and I love the whole 80% of the money

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:47.190
wasn't going to the executives of it

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:48.420
and people weren't on the board

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:50.520
'cause they just wanted to rub
elbows with other important,

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:51.980
all the things I became like oh my God,

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:53.513
this is what NGOs are about.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:56.090
So that was exciting.

00:15:56.090 --> 00:15:57.610
But the third bucket for me

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:01.533
is I'm unbelievably
passionate about helping

00:16:01.533 --> 00:16:05.240
people that I even
slightly've met three times

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:07.997
when they have cancer or their house,

00:16:07.997 --> 00:16:10.070
again, I'm looking at
Tyler 'cause he knows,

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:12.040
this is something I've
never said publicly,

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:12.940
the most interesting thing

00:16:12.940 --> 00:16:15.610
about how I think about
it is I feel guilty

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:20.080
to only do macro things like
cure the water issue in Africa

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:22.120
when somebody I've had dinner with

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:25.600
three times around business has a GoFundMe

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.550
'cause she has breast cancer.

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:30.127
I'm like, fuck, I have to give to that.

00:16:30.127 --> 00:16:31.990
I don't want to be ideolog,

00:16:31.990 --> 00:16:34.520
people love to do things
for their public pers,

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:36.430
the reason I don't talk about non-profit

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:38.400
is 'cause I watch all
my other friends use it

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.530
to tell the world that
they're a good person.

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:41.800
- Well, I don't mind talking about it

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:42.990
if it's gonna do some good.

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:44.012
- I agree.

00:16:44.012 --> 00:16:45.810
- So we started a thing
called Reaching Out Montclair

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:48.140
which just helps the
underserved in Montclair.

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:50.880
It will never go outside
of Montclair, New Jersey.

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:52.300
And we see it first on.

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:54.410
We got kids costumes this weekend,

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:55.944
we had authors come and read books.

00:16:55.944 --> 00:16:57.720
We get people mattresses.
- It's real.

00:16:57.720 --> 00:16:59.940
And Montclair's an interesting comp.

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:02.760
Montclair and Summit has
this and other places,

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:05.830
I'm very fascinated by towns

00:17:05.830 --> 00:17:09.790
that really have the
"other side of the tracks."

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:13.070
It's a very micro dynamic that

00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:14.470
what I loved about when I saw that,

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:15.370
I looked at it for a few minutes,

00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:16.203
I look at nothing,

00:17:16.203 --> 00:17:17.410
I'm like oh, I like that

00:17:17.410 --> 00:17:18.690
because I was always weirded out

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:20.720
by the extreme,

00:17:20.720 --> 00:17:24.210
I did so much direct mail for Wine Library

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:25.910
when I was building that
store in New Jersey,

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:28.530
I know everything about
every zip code in New Jersey.

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:31.270
And so it's so funny when I meet people,

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:32.250
they're like, oh, I'm from Bergan,

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:33.083
I'm like, which town?

00:17:33.083 --> 00:17:34.906
And I'm like, I know that.

00:17:34.906 --> 00:17:37.300
113, median income.

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:38.740
I know stuff about Jersey

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:41.510
and Summit, Montclair,
couple other places,

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:44.070
they're fascinating to me, Westfield,

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:47.470
where you have these big micro climates,

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:49.130
I'm using wine terminology

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:51.920
where this side is like
homes that are a million

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:53.340
and that side is really not.

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:55.190
- And our kids all go to
school in the same place.

00:17:55.190 --> 00:17:56.550
My kids went to the public school--

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:57.930
- Which oh by the way is amazing.

00:17:57.930 --> 00:17:58.885
- Yeah.

00:17:58.885 --> 00:18:00.160
- Which is the greatest thing for the kids

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:01.783
on the good side of the track.

00:18:01.783 --> 00:18:03.630
- Yeah, Montclair High to
Stanford, the first kid.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:04.898
- Love it.

00:18:04.898 --> 00:18:08.830
School's broken in America, quick take.

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:10.560
It's so fucking broken

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:11.693
that I can't breath.

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:14.710
It's so broken.

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:15.920
Does anybody understand

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:17.290
the world we actually live in?

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:18.123
- Yes.

00:18:18.123 --> 00:18:20.810
Well I think college is the
worst thing in the world,

00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:22.810
that kids can't afford to go to college.

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:24.730
You need to start a college.

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:27.120
You need to start a free college.

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:29.270
I think that would
probably be your legacy.

00:18:30.555 --> 00:18:32.230
- It's funny, when you said 70,

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:33.730
I'm like, I'm gonna fuck up college,

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:35.542
it ran through my mind.

00:18:35.542 --> 00:18:37.000
- You didn't graduate or you did?

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:37.833
- Yes I did

00:18:37.833 --> 00:18:39.683
but I went to Mount Ida College.

00:18:40.596 --> 00:18:42.235
I majored in--
- So what's wrong with that?

00:18:42.235 --> 00:18:43.538
- What's wrong with it?

00:18:43.538 --> 00:18:47.270
Literally the mic that we're talking into

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:48.323
could get admitted.

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:51.410
Literally if you're,

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:54.230
dead people can get
into Mount Ida College.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:56.432
Actually, Mount Ida College
went out of business recently.

00:18:56.432 --> 00:18:57.930
This is a fun fact,

00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:01.290
literally went out of business
after 100 plus, 200 years.

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:04.286
I think UMass Amherst like absorbed it.

00:19:04.286 --> 00:19:05.830
What's wrong with it?

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:10.060
I played Madden for 98%
of my college experience.

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:11.647
- And you got an A in
Madden so that's good.

00:19:11.647 --> 00:19:13.052
I went to Emerson College.

00:19:13.052 --> 00:19:15.570
I showed up the day before
with my transcripts,

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:16.430
they let me in.

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:17.770
They said, "What do you want to major in?"

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:18.603
I said, "Makeup."

00:19:18.603 --> 00:19:19.550
They said, "Okay."

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:20.710
I said, "Do you have a makeup program?"

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:21.700
They said, "No."

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:23.690
I said, "Alright, I'll make it up."

00:19:23.690 --> 00:19:24.523
